WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.455
Come along, little seed. There is a whole world waiting beyond this station.

00:00:08.749 --> 00:00:12.256
But where could you make a home? Let us follow the clues.

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:20.662
Before a forest, before a river, we need a little ground.

00:00:22.807 --> 00:00:29.322
Layers of noise lift the hills. Frequency changes their size; resolution reveals their shape.

00:00:31.467 --> 00:00:35.405
A shader gives snow, stone, and shore their own voice.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:43.590
Listen. Here comes the rain. Each drop begins a small journey downhill.

00:00:46.244 --> 00:00:50.667
Together, the drops carry soil away, then leave it somewhere new.

00:00:53.321 --> 00:00:58.896
Near the coast, tides cover the low ground. Even a familiar path can disappear.

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:06.697
Our little seed has found land. But land alone is not enough.

00:01:11.014 --> 00:01:17.233
Too steep? Too dry? Warmth and moisture turn a map into a patchwork of possible homes.

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:28.073
Ah, this sheltered patch will do. A branch grows, then branches again.

00:01:30.093 --> 00:01:36.040
A tree becomes larger. A forest becomes more crowded. Those are two different stories.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:43.530
Growth, new seeds, and competition decide which story comes next.

00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:49.442
But wait. What if our world has an inside?

00:01:51.739 --> 00:01:57.824
Density tells us where solid ground exists. Subtract a shape, and a cave appears.

00:02:00.121 --> 00:02:06.253
Meshing reveals its surface. Chunks let us change one piece without rebuilding the whole world.

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:14.289
A breeze catches another seed. The invisible has left a visible trail.

00:02:16.372 --> 00:02:21.366
A vector gives each place a direction and a speed. Particles show us the journey.

00:02:23.449 --> 00:02:28.467
A fluid needs another step: pressure correction keeps its flow more consistent.

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:37.424
So a world is a conversation: between shape, water, climate, and life.

00:02:38.402 --> 00:02:45.556
Our map joins the ideas. The experiments remain separate, so we can see what each rule really does.

00:02:46.535 --> 00:02:49.571
Now, let us change one thing at a time.

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:56.211
Keep the seed. Keep the camera. Change one rule, and watch.

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:05.658
Watch the frequency slider move. Broad hills become smaller, more closely spaced hills.

00:03:08.492 --> 00:03:13.716
The seed stays the same. We are changing the pattern, not rolling a new world.

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:21.511
Now keep the field, and change how many points we sample.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:27.900
More cells reveal detail. They also cost more work.

00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:35.778
Only the snow threshold moves. The mountain itself stays still.

00:03:37.346 --> 00:03:40.982
A material can explain height without changing geometry.

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:48.938
Both landscapes begin alike. Both receive the same number of simulation steps.

00:03:50.907 --> 00:03:56.632
One gets more rain. Ochre shows removed ground; teal shows deposited sediment.

00:03:58.601 --> 00:04:02.581
The measurements help us notice changes that a pretty image might hide.

00:04:05.550 --> 00:04:09.507
Hold the terrain and temperature still. Let moisture increase.

00:04:12.791 --> 00:04:19.266
The habitat labels change. This is a climate classification, not a weather forecast.

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:28.777
A sphere, a box, and a noisy volume begin with different density rules.

00:04:31.185 --> 00:04:36.142
The slice reveals solid and empty space. Marching cubes follows their boundary.

00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:44.230
Watch the cave radius. A small cutter opens a tunnel through the mountain.

00:04:47.993 --> 00:04:54.787
Now change the order. Carve first, add a pillar afterward, and solid returns inside the opening.

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:05.414
One pocket touches nearby chunks. Their meshes rebuild; distant meshes remain.

00:05:06.681 --> 00:05:09.283
Local work saves unnecessary work.

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:16.893
Increase the voxel resolution. The same rule now produces more triangles.

00:05:18.391 --> 00:05:23.052
Compare measured meshing time, not just how smooth the surface looks.

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:29.903
Smaller chunks can limit an edit, but there are more pieces to manage.

00:05:32.252 --> 00:05:34.201
The counters reveal the tradeoff.

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:42.339
Compare the alternatives here. This project implements marching cubes.

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:49.899
Two particle groups travel for the same time, at the same speed.

00:05:53.052 --> 00:05:59.398
Turn one field by ninety degrees. Its direction changes; the travel distance does not.

00:06:03.550 --> 00:06:07.766
Both grids carry dye. The right grid also corrects its pressure.

00:06:12.191 --> 00:06:18.125
Watch the divergence measurement fall. Dye makes motion visible, but it is not water height.

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:28.184
Replace each forward instruction with the same little branching sentence.

00:06:30.369 --> 00:06:38.365
Five segments become twenty-five, then one hundred and twenty-five. Grammar depth builds structure; it is not age.

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:45.862
Keep the same branches. A wider turn angle opens the crown.

00:06:49.550 --> 00:06:55.264
Reproduction is off. Growth changes size, while the population stays fixed.

00:06:57.550 --> 00:07:00.478
Now allow the right forest to make new seeds.

00:07:02.759 --> 00:07:07.148
Both stands can grow, but only one can welcome new seedlings.

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:16.269
Watch births, deaths, and total population. A forest is more than a collection of tree shapes.

00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:23.851
Crowding can lower health before trees die. Watch the health measurement.

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:33.029
The forest holds still while the tide rises. Low ground disappears beneath the water.

00:07:34.962 --> 00:07:39.338
The path search must find dry ground, or admit there is no route.

00:07:41.271 --> 00:07:44.617
As the tide retreats, a connection can return.

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:51.024
And here we are again, with a whole world in front of us.

00:07:53.490 --> 00:08:00.255
The turning planet brings the ideas together. Its controls do not run one giant physical simulation.

00:08:02.722 --> 00:08:09.702
Our seed has taught us how to ask better questions: what changed, what stayed fixed, and why?

00:08:12.169 --> 00:08:16.083
Now it is your turn. Which little rule will you change first?
